<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>6.4.2: Finding the GCF of Two or More Expressions</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01629</md:content-id>
    <md:title>6.4.2: Finding the GCF of Two or More Expressions</md:title>
    <md:uuid>9907a6ed-1591-4800-b917-49b094fcc04f</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity (25 minutes)</title>
<para>This activity uses several different methods to introduce students to finding the greatest common factor (GCF) of two
  or more expressions. Students will factor each expression into all of its prime factors. They will compare these lists
  of factors to determine which ones they all have in common. By multiplying these common factors together, students
  will find their GCF.</para>
<title>Launch</title>
<para>Start the lesson by introducing vocabulary that will be helpful:</para>
<para>Factoring an expression:</para>
<list>
  <item> Factoring is splitting a product into its prime factors. If these factors are multiplied together, the result
    will be the original product. </item>
</list>
<para>Greatest common factor:</para>
<list>
  <item> The greatest common factor (GCF) of two or more expressions is the largest expression that is a factor of all the
    expressions. </item>
</list>
<para>Students will become more familiar with these terms as the lesson progresses.</para>
<para>Prepare sheets of paper with the expressions \(120m^3n^2p^4\) and \(80m^2ns^4\) written separately on individual
  pieces. Prepare a set of these expressions for every pair of students in your class.</para>
<para>Arrange the students into pairs for the activity. Hand out one expression, \(120m^3n^2p^4\), to one student of the
  pair and the other expression, \(80m^2ns^4\), to the other student. Instruct the students not to share their
  expression with anyone. Read the instructions for the activity as a class and address any confusion around the rules.
  Allow 5 minutes for the pairs to complete the activity. After the activity is complete, share the expressions that
  were used and their GCF, \(40m^2n\).</para>
<para>Spend 5 minutes reviewing the next example as a class. Walk the students through each step, stopping to address any
  concerns. Then allow the remainder of the time for students to complete the exercises individually. With any
  additional time, answer any questions students may have about finding the GCF of these expressions.</para>
<note class="extra-support">
  <title>Support for Students with Disabilities</title><para><!--Extra Support Name-->Representation: Illustrate Connections</para>
  <!--Support Content--><para>Use different colors to help show the common factors across two or more expressions.  Students can find and highlight the factors of each expression only when they occur in all of the expressions.  
</para>
    <para><emphasis effect="italics"><!--Support Italics-->Supports accessibility for Organizing, Visual-Spatial Processing</emphasis></para></note>

<title>Student Activity </title>
<para>For the first part of the activity, you will work with a partner. Your teacher will hand out two pieces of paper, one
  to each of you. It is important for the activity that you do not show your partner your paper.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item> When you receive your paper, look at your expression. Write down all of the factors in your expression. </item>
  <para>If your expression was \(100x^3y^2\), you would write down \(2 \cdot 2 \cdot 5 \cdot 5 \cdot x \cdot x \cdot x
    \cdot y \cdot y\). Do not share these factors with your partner yet.</para>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>
    When you and your partner have finished finding the factors of your expression, you will try to determine the GCF of
    these two terms without actually knowing the other’s expression!
    Take turns with your partner sharing one factor and the quantity of that factor. For example, you could say,
    “My expression has two factors that are 5” or “My expression has three factors that are
    \(x\).” Write down the factors your partner has shared with you.
  </item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>
    Once you and your partner have finished taking turns sharing factors, you should know all of the factors in your
    partner’s expression. Using this information, determine your partner’s expression and find the GCF of
    these two expressions.
     Once you have found the GCF, compare your answer with your partner’s. Did you have the same answer?
  </item>
</list>
<para>Write the GCF that you found.</para>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis>
  Expression A: \(120m^3n^2p^4\)</para>
<para>Factors of Expression A: \(2 \cdot 2 \cdot 2 \cdot 3 \cdot 5 \cdot m \cdot m \cdot m \cdot n \cdot n \cdot p \cdot p
  \cdot p \cdot p\)</para>
<para>Expression B: \(80m^2ns^4\)</para>
<para>Factors of Expression B: \(2 \cdot 2 \cdot 2 \cdot 2 \cdot 5 \cdot m \cdot m \cdot n \cdot s \cdot s \cdot s \cdot
  s\)</para>

<para>GCF: \(2 \cdot 2 \cdot 2 \cdot 5 \cdot m \cdot m \cdot n = 40m^2n\)</para>
<para>Now, let’s look at an of example finding the GCF of two expressions as a class:</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item>\(18g^4h^2, 63g^3h^3\)
  </item>
</list>
<para>Write down your answer. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis></para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1 - 
  </emphasis>Factor each coefficient into primes. Write all variables with exponents in expanded form.</para>
<para>  \(18g^4h^2 = 3 \cdot 3 \cdot 2 \cdot g \cdot g \cdot g \cdot g \cdot h \cdot h\)</para>
<para>\(63g^3h^3 = 3 \cdot 3 \cdot 7 \cdot g \cdot g \cdot g \cdot h \cdot h \cdot h\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> List all factors, matching common factors in a column. In each column, circle the common factors.</para>
<para><figure><media alt=""><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/3a29f2968fa8a3391a37ee0d5a26bec3c4d9e491"/></media></figure></para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Bring down the common factors that all expressions share.</para>
<para> GCF = \(3 \cdot 3 \cdot g \cdot g \cdot g \cdot h \cdot h\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 4 -</emphasis> Multiply the factors.</para>
<para>  GCF = \(9g^3h^2\)</para>
<para>Work individually on the next part of the activity.</para>
<para>Find the greatest common factor of each of the following sets of expressions.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
  <item> \(10p^3q, 12pq^2\) </item>
</list>
<para> <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(2pq\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="6" type="enumerated">
  <item> \(8a^2b^3, 10ab^2\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(2ab^2\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="7" type="enumerated">
  <item> \(10a^3, 12a^2, 14a\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(2a\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="8" type="enumerated">
  <item> \(35x^3y^2, 10x^4y, 5x^5y^3\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(5x^3y\)</para>
<title>Anticipated Misconceptions</title>
<para>As students play the game with their partner, it is important that, in the first step, they correctly factor their
  original expression into its prime factors. Monitor for those who do not completely factor each expression into all of
  its prime factors. Challenge students to analyze each factor they have found and be sure that each factor cannot be
  factored any further.</para>
<title>Activity Synthesis</title>
<para>Ask students how they were able to determine their partner’s expression in the game without actually knowing
  what it was. (By multiplying all of the factors their partner provided, they could find their partner’s
  expression.)</para>
<para>Ask a student to explain in their own words how to find the GCF of two different expressions. Use the list below to
  help the student as needed:</para>
<list>
  <item> Step 1. Factor each coefficient into primes. Write all variables with exponents in expanded form. </item>
  <item> Step 2. List all factors, matching common factors in a column. In each column, circle the common factors. </item>
  <item> Step 3. Bring down the common factors that all expressions share. </item>
  <item> Step 4. Multiply the factors. </item>
</list>
<title> 6.4.2: Self Check</title>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold"><emphasis>After the activity, students will answer the following question to check their
    understanding of the concepts explored in the activity.</emphasis></para>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">QUESTION:</para>
<para>Find the greatest common factor of \(25m^4, 35m^3, 20m^2\).</para>
<table class="os-raise-textheavytable">
  <caption>
  </caption>
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">Answers</entry>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">Feedback</entry>
    </row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>
        \(10m^2\)
      </entry>
      <entry>
        Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: Each expression does not share the factor 2, so the GCF cannot
        include 10. The answer is \(5m^2\).
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        \(5m\)
      </entry>
      <entry>
        Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: Each expression has at least two factors of \(m\), so the GCF
        will have \(m^2\). The answer is \(5m^2\).
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        \(5m^2\)
      </entry>
      <entry>
        That’s correct! Check yourself: All of these expressions share the factors \(5 \cdot m \cdot m\).
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        \(10m\)
      </entry>
      <entry>
        Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: Each expression does not share the factor 2, so the GCF cannot
        include 10. Each expression has at least two factors of \(m\), so the GCF will have \(m^2\). The answer is
        \(5m^2\).
      </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>

<title>6.4.2: Additional Resources</title>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold"><emphasis>The following content is available to students who would like more support based on
    their experience with the self check. Students will not automatically have access to this content, so you may wish
    to share it with those who could benefit from it.</emphasis></para>
<title>Finding the GCF of Two or More Expressions</title>
<para>Earlier, we multiplied factors together to get a product. Now, we will reverse this process; we will start with a
  product and then break it down into its factors. Splitting a product into factors is called factoring.</para>
<para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/2ea10811016c35eedea3a00116dc7a5d2a0a685b"/></media></figure></para>
<para>Now we will factor expressions and find the greatest common factor of two or more expressions.</para>
<note>
  <para class="os-raise-text-bold">GREATEST COMMON FACTOR </para>
  <hr/>
  <para>The greatest common factor (GCF) of two or more expressions is the largest expression that is a factor of all the
    expressions.</para>
</note>

<para>We will summarize the steps we use to find the greatest common factor.</para>

<para>How to find the greatest common factor (GCF) of two expressions:</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis><emphasis> -</emphasis> Factor each coefficient into primes. Write all variables with exponents in expanded form.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis><emphasis> -</emphasis> List all factors, matching common factors in a column. In each column, circle the common factors.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis><emphasis> -</emphasis> Bring down the common factors that all expressions share.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis><emphasis> -</emphasis> Multiply the factors.</para>
<para>The following example will show the steps to find the greatest common factor of three expressions.</para>


  <para class="os-raise-text-bold">Example</para>

<para>Find the greatest common factor of \(21x^3, 9x^2, 15x\).</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Factor each coefficient into primes and write the variables with exponents in expanded form.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2 - 
  </emphasis>Circle the common factors in each column.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Bring down the common factors. </para>
<para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/3bed9311fc9b961e59a3086767cf584b0ef99737"/></media></figure></para>
<para>Step 4 - 
  Multiply the factors.</para>
<para>  GFC = \(3x\)</para>
<para>The GCF of \(21x^3\), \(9x^2\), and \(15x\) is \(3x\).</para>
<title>Try It: Finding the GCF of Two or More Expressions</title>
<para>Find the greatest common factor of \(14x^3, 105x, 70x^2\).</para>
<para>Write down your answer. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
<title>Solution</title>
<para>Here is how to find the GCF of multiple expressions.</para>
<para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/60f313f41a2b4ded40b13e05b9ab3273bde7273d "/></media></figure></para>
<para>The GCF of these expressions is \(7x\).</para></section>
